# ==== Purpose ====
#
# Test the hand shake between the flush stage header thread and the dedicated
# binlog flush thread of the parallel binlog flush feature
# (ordered_commit_flush_binlog_in_advance).
#
# The binlog flush thread is not allowed to write binlog into the IO cache
# beyond the point covered by the storage engines' logs already flushed to
# disk. When the IO cache of the binlog file has no room left for the next
# event, it calls MYSQL_BIN_LOG::suspend_till_ha_flushed(), announces itself,
# and blocks on m_ha_flushed_cond until the header thread has run
# ha_flush_logs() and published that fact with set_ha_flushed_status(true).
#
# The announcement and the flag are plain seq_cst atomics, not lock protected
# (the lock only keeps the wakeup from being broadcast before the waiter has
# blocked), so the interesting part of the protocol is the interleaving of the
# two threads. This test reproduces each window deterministically with
# DEBUG_SYNC points placed in suspend_till_ha_flushed():
#
#   binlog_ha_flushed_before_lock        before the worker takes the lock
#   binlog_ha_flushed_after_announce     announced, not yet checked the flag
#   binlog_ha_flushed_before_cond_wait   flag seen false, about to block
#
# and checks that no interleaving loses the wakeup or deadlocks. A lost wakeup
# shows up as a hanging COMMIT, i.e. a test timeout, because the flush stage
# header thread would wait forever on cond_flush_done.
#
# The publication of the flag is delayed by the debug flag
# binlog_flush_delay_ha_flushed_publish where the test needs the flush thread
# to check the flag before the header thread sets it; otherwise the ordering of
# the two threads depends on how long ha_flush_logs() takes compared to writing
# one IO cache worth of events.
#
# What this test checks of the memory ordering: the pairing of the two
# accesses is a store-buffer (Dekker) pattern. On the header side the flag is
# stored and the announcement is loaded; on the flush thread side the
# announcement is stored and the flag is loaded. x86 (TSO) keeps stores in
# order and keeps loads in order, but it does reorder a store with a later
# load, so either side can have its store delayed past its own load and both
# sides can then read the other's store as unset. Scenario 3 parks the flush
# thread right after a load that saw the flag unset, so the header side pair
# runs while it is parked, which is why a weakened order of these accesses can
# lose the wakeup here and not only on ARM. Scenario 2 is not an ordering
# check of this kind: the sleep there is long enough for the stores to drain
# before the flush thread loads the flag, so what it covers is the lock
# ordering around m_ha_flushed_lock. The ordering itself remains a race that
# the hardware has to lose; the deterministic check of it is left to code
# review of the four accesses in set_ha_flushed_status() /
# suspend_till_ha_flushed(), which must all be seq_cst.
#
# A transaction triggers the hand shake when the events it writes are larger
# than the IO cache of the binlog file (IO_SIZE * 8 = 32KiB). With
# binlog_format=ROW, the row events of a 100000 bytes blob are written to the
# file in the flush stage and cross that boundary, so every transaction below
# suspends at least once.
#
# ==== Implementation ====
#
# The DEBUG_SYNC points are hit by the binlog flush thread, but with the THD of
# the flush stage header thread as current_thd (binlog_flush() runs with that
# THD, see Thd_backup_and_restore). The actions are therefore installed by con1
# -- the session whose COMMIT becomes the leader of the flush stage -- and the
# signals are sent from a second connection, con2.
#
# Do not park the header thread itself: it would share one debug_sync_control
# with the flush thread, which hits its points with the header thread's THD.
# Concurrent debug_sync() calls would race on its hit counter, and
# debug_sync_remove_action() would move the action array while the parked
# thread still points into it. That is why the header thread side is delayed by
# a debug flag (binlog_flush_delay_ha_flushed_publish) rather than parked on a
# synchronization point of its own.
#
# Each scenario waits for its result on both sides: con2 asserts that the
# flush thread reached the expected synchronization point (a warning is what a
# timeout on that wait produces), and con1 asserts after the reap that its
# COMMIT produced no warnings, which is what a timeout of the flush thread's
# own wait would leave behind.
#
# ==== Coverage ====
#
#   scenario 1  the header thread publishes before the flush thread announces
#   scenario 2  the flush thread has announced, but not checked the flag yet,
#               when the header thread publishes and finds it
#   scenario 3  the flush thread has checked the flag when the header thread
#               publishes, so it blocks and must be woken up by a broadcast
#   scenario 4  the window of scenario 3 over several consecutive groups
#   scenario 5  concurrent commits, no synchronization points
#
# ==== References ====
#
# The hand shake is set_ha_flushed_status() / suspend_till_ha_flushed() in
# sql/binlog.h.

--source include/have_log_bin.inc
--source include/have_debug.inc
--source include/have_debug_sync.inc

--let $oc_flush_in_advance= `SELECT @@GLOBAL.ordered_commit_flush_binlog_in_advance`
if (`SELECT $oc_flush_in_advance = 0`)
{
  --skip Test requires ordered_commit_flush_binlog_in_advance (Hygon CPU)
}

--let $payload_size= 100000

# Seconds to wait before letting the flush thread continue, so that the header
# thread has published the flag in the meantime. Must exceed the delay of the
# binlog_flush_delay_ha_flushed_publish debug point (0.5 seconds, see
# fetch_and_process_flush_stage_queue() in sql/binlog.cc) for the scenarios
# which use it, or else the header thread is still sleeping when the signal
# below arrives and the interleaving that gets exercised is a different one
# (the test does not fail in that case, it just covers less).
--let $publish_wait= 1

# Seconds to wait for a synchronization point to be hit.
--let $sync_timeout= 60

RESET MASTER;
CREATE TABLE t1 (id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT, pad LONGBLOB) ENGINE=InnoDB;

connect (con1, localhost, root,,);
connect (con2, localhost, root,,);
connect (con3, localhost, root,,);

--echo #
--echo # Scenario 1: the header thread publishes ha_flushed before the flush
--echo # thread announces itself. The flush thread must see the published flag
--echo # when it announces itself and must not block.
--echo #
--echo # The flush thread is parked before it takes the lock, i.e. before it can
--echo # be seen by the header thread, and the header thread only needs to run
--echo # ha_flush_logs() to publish the flag. Waiting here, instead of resuming
--echo # the flush thread immediately, lets the header thread get there first by
--echo # a margin of many orders of magnitude, so that this ordering and not the
--echo # opposite one is the one exercised. If ha_flush_logs() were so slow that
--echo # it did not make it in time, the flush thread would find the flag unset
--echo # and block, i.e. the run would degenerate into scenario 3 instead of
--echo # failing.
--echo #
--connection con1
--eval SET DEBUG_SYNC='binlog_ha_flushed_before_lock SIGNAL worker_before_lock WAIT_FOR resume_before_lock TIMEOUT $sync_timeout'
BEGIN;
--eval INSERT INTO t1 (pad) VALUES (REPEAT('a', $payload_size))
--send COMMIT

--connection con2
--eval SET DEBUG_SYNC='now WAIT_FOR worker_before_lock TIMEOUT $sync_timeout'
--source include/assert_no_warnings.inc
--eval SELECT SLEEP($publish_wait)
SET DEBUG_SYNC='now SIGNAL resume_before_lock';

--connection con1
--reap
--source include/assert_no_warnings.inc

--echo #
--echo # Scenario 2: the flush thread has announced itself, but has not checked
--echo # the flag yet, when the header thread publishes it. The header thread
--echo # finds the announcement and blocks on m_ha_flushed_lock, which the flush
--echo # thread holds while it is parked. The flush thread then sees the flag
--echo # set, cancels its announcement and releases the lock, and the header
--echo # thread finds nobody left to wake. Neither side may block forever.
--echo #
--echo # The publication is delayed, as in scenario 3, so that the flush thread
--echo # has announced itself before the header thread publishes. Without the
--echo # delay the header thread would normally publish before the flush thread
--echo # announces itself, and the run would degenerate into scenario 1 instead
--echo # of failing.
--echo #
--connection con1
SET SESSION debug='+d,binlog_flush_delay_ha_flushed_publish';
SET DEBUG_SYNC='RESET';
--eval SET DEBUG_SYNC='binlog_ha_flushed_after_announce SIGNAL worker_after_announce WAIT_FOR resume_after_announce TIMEOUT $sync_timeout'
BEGIN;
--eval INSERT INTO t1 (pad) VALUES (REPEAT('b', $payload_size))
--send COMMIT

--connection con2
--eval SET DEBUG_SYNC='now WAIT_FOR worker_after_announce TIMEOUT $sync_timeout'
--source include/assert_no_warnings.inc
--eval SELECT SLEEP($publish_wait)
SET DEBUG_SYNC='now SIGNAL resume_after_announce';

--connection con1
--reap
--source include/assert_no_warnings.inc
SET SESSION debug='-d,binlog_flush_delay_ha_flushed_publish';

--echo #
--echo # Scenario 3: the flush thread announces itself and finds ha_flushed
--echo # unset. The header thread publishes the flag, sees the announcement and
--echo # blocks on m_ha_flushed_lock, which the flush thread holds. The flush
--echo # thread must then block on the condition variable, release the lock, and
--echo # be woken up by the broadcast of the header thread.
--echo #
--echo # Whether the flush thread gets to check the flag before the header thread
--echo # publishes it depends on how long ha_flush_logs() takes compared to the
--echo # writing of one IO cache worth of events, so the publication is delayed
--echo # with a debug flag to make the flush thread reach the check first. The
--echo # sleep below then lets the header thread publish and block on the lock,
--echo # which the flush thread holds while it is parked at the sync point.
--echo #
--connection con1
SET SESSION debug='+d,binlog_flush_delay_ha_flushed_publish';
SET DEBUG_SYNC='RESET';
--eval SET DEBUG_SYNC='binlog_ha_flushed_before_cond_wait SIGNAL worker_before_cond_wait WAIT_FOR resume_cond_wait TIMEOUT $sync_timeout'
BEGIN;
--eval INSERT INTO t1 (pad) VALUES (REPEAT('c', $payload_size))
--send COMMIT

--connection con2
--eval SET DEBUG_SYNC='now WAIT_FOR worker_before_cond_wait TIMEOUT $sync_timeout'
--source include/assert_no_warnings.inc
--eval SELECT SLEEP($publish_wait)
SET DEBUG_SYNC='now SIGNAL resume_cond_wait';

--connection con1
--reap
--source include/assert_no_warnings.inc
SET SESSION debug='-d,binlog_flush_delay_ha_flushed_publish';

--echo #
--echo # Scenario 4: the window of scenario 3 over several consecutive groups.
--echo # The header thread clears is_ha_flushed before handing the next group
--echo # over, so the flush thread must announce and block again for every
--echo # group; a missing clear would make the flush thread skip the wait and
--echo # the WAIT_FOR below time out.
--echo #
--let $iteration= 1
while ($iteration <= 5)
{
  --connection con1
  SET SESSION debug='+d,binlog_flush_delay_ha_flushed_publish';
  SET DEBUG_SYNC='RESET';
  --eval SET DEBUG_SYNC='binlog_ha_flushed_before_cond_wait SIGNAL worker_before_cond_wait WAIT_FOR resume_cond_wait TIMEOUT $sync_timeout'
  BEGIN;
  --eval INSERT INTO t1 (pad) VALUES (REPEAT('d', $payload_size))
  --send COMMIT

  --connection con2
  --eval SET DEBUG_SYNC='now WAIT_FOR worker_before_cond_wait TIMEOUT $sync_timeout'
  --source include/assert_no_warnings.inc
  --eval SELECT SLEEP($publish_wait)
  SET DEBUG_SYNC='now SIGNAL resume_cond_wait';

  --connection con1
  --reap
  --source include/assert_no_warnings.inc
  SET SESSION debug='-d,binlog_flush_delay_ha_flushed_publish';
  --inc $iteration
}

--echo #
--echo # Scenario 5: concurrent commits, without synchronization points, so that
--echo # groups of more than one transaction and arbitrary interleavings of the
--echo # header and the flush thread are exercised.
--echo #
--let $iteration= 1
while ($iteration <= 10)
{
  --connection con1
  --send_eval INSERT INTO t1 (pad) VALUES (REPEAT('e', $payload_size))
  --connection con2
  --send_eval INSERT INTO t1 (pad) VALUES (REPEAT('f', $payload_size))
  --connection con3
  --send_eval INSERT INTO t1 (pad) VALUES (REPEAT('g', $payload_size))
  --connection con1
  --reap
  --connection con2
  --reap
  --connection con3
  --reap
  --inc $iteration
}

--connection default

--let $count= `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t1`
--let $total_size= `SELECT SUM(LENGTH(pad)) FROM t1`
--let $expected_count= 38
--let $expected_size= `SELECT 38 * $payload_size`
--let $assert_text= All transactions were committed
--let $assert_cond= $count = $expected_count AND $total_size = $expected_size
--source include/assert.inc
SELECT COUNT(*), SUM(LENGTH(pad)) FROM t1;

# The rows above come from the storage engine. Read the binary log back as
# well: the events the flush thread wrote while it was suspended must form a
# valid event stream. mysqlbinlog verifies the checksum of every event and
# returns a non-zero status if the stream does not parse, which fails the test.
--let $binlog_file= query_get_value(SHOW MASTER STATUS, File, 1)
--let $MYSQLD_DATADIR= `SELECT @@datadir`
FLUSH LOGS;
--exec $MYSQL_BINLOG --verify-binlog-checksum $MYSQLD_DATADIR/$binlog_file > $MYSQLTEST_VARDIR/tmp/binlog_flush_thread_handshake.sql
# Every transaction must also be present in the binary log, not only in the
# table: this counts the transactions the flush thread wrote and compares the
# number with the one expected above through the result file.
--echo # Transactions found in the binary log:
--exec grep -c "^BEGIN" $MYSQLTEST_VARDIR/tmp/binlog_flush_thread_handshake.sql
--remove_file $MYSQLTEST_VARDIR/tmp/binlog_flush_thread_handshake.sql

--connection con1
SET DEBUG_SYNC='RESET';
--connection con2
SET DEBUG_SYNC='RESET';
--connection con3
SET DEBUG_SYNC='RESET';

--connection default
disconnect con1;
disconnect con2;
disconnect con3;
DROP TABLE t1;
